    "What is your relationship with mathematics?": "I see math as a tool for understanding the world, not a rigid set of rules. Growing up with parents who both have PhDs, I developed a curiosity-driven approach, but I also appreciate how it connects to real-life problems like physics and economics. It’s practical and creative, not just abstract theory.",
    "Do you ever get anxious when thinking about mathematics?": "I rarely feel anxious about math because I’ve built confidence through consistent practice and seeing its applications. My background in physics and history helps me approach problems contextually, so I focus on understanding concepts rather than memorizing steps. Anxiety only arises when I’m stuck on something too complex, but I usually overcome it with patience.",
    "Did you ever use AI to support your math learning in the last year? If yes, how was your experience?": "Yes, I used AI tools like Wolfram Alpha and ChatGPT to check my work on calculus problems. They helped me verify solutions quickly and explain steps in simpler terms. I found them very useful for understanding where I might have gone wrong, but I still prefer hands-on practice to build intuition.",
    "How would you explain, step by step, how to solve a second order algebraic equation?": "First, write the equation in standard form: ax² + bx + c = 0. Then, calculate the discriminant (b² - 4ac). If it’s positive, there are two real solutions; if zero, one real solution; if negative, two complex solutions. Finally, use the quadratic formula: x = [-b ± √(discriminant)] / (2a). This method works for all quadratic equations.",
    "How would you explain, step by step, how to find the stationary points of an equation y=f(x)?": "To find stationary points, take the first derivative of f(x), set it equal to zero, and solve for x. These x-values are critical points. Then, plug them back into f(x) to find the corresponding y-values. You can also check if they’re maxima or minima by using the second derivative test.",
